Replay the paired full-size H821 request diagnostic for issue #44. Slurm job
7767 on h100-nvl-1-0-cc ran 89e1df4 and 9d00a32 consecutively on CPU 0.
Both executables used Bend 2.0.40 and Ubuntu Clang 18.1.3. The guest exposed
GenuineIntel family 6, model 207, stepping 2 (06/cf) with one thread per core.
Each process reported one worker, returned zero, and matched all four exact
closing lines with its own build ID. The proof SHA-256 was
efa762e20f72b64e183ad6f688eccf5b2b79649928d8e3a2a96645645dd08af9.

From the checkout containing these patches, create two scratch worktrees:

    repo=$(pwd)
    patch_dir=$repo/research/spikes/request
    run=$(mktemp -d)
    git worktree add --detach "$run/before" 89e1df4
    git worktree add --detach "$run/after" 9d00a32
    git -C "$run/before" apply "$patch_dir/outer-timers.patch"
    git -C "$run/after" apply --exclude=src/lin_own.bend "$patch_dir/outer-timers.patch"
    git -C "$run/after" apply "$patch_dir/third-lin-timers.patch"
    for side in before after; do
      (cd "$run/$side" && make bootstrap upstream standalone && (
        f=.upstream/FlashProve/examples/full-size
        id=$(cat "$f/ID.txt")
        env PATH='' ./build/verify-bend "$f/config" "$f/ivk" "$f/example.req" \
          "$f/example.y" "$f/example.proof" -id "$id" -data build -j 1 -timing \
          > "$run/$side.stdout" 2> "$run/$side.stderr"
      )) || break
    done
    python3 "$patch_dir/tally-outer.py" "$run/before.stderr" "$run/after.stderr"

For the original Linux affinity, prefix the env command with taskset -c 0.
The instrumented build IDs were
before 6e3c25a2d288784b939009a0c91db4c183913d7aa2c78a3cc3fad9c21aaf433d
after  7c7d84c2f514dff1145d77f918a15c59dfe19f4f3a8936a1d0355d6de7b54e5a
The new hunk moves the same three markers to the projected LIN call. All
other clock boundaries use the original patch. Consecutive differences sum
exactly to each request timer. The resulting table is frozen at
docs/research/notes/third-request-outer.tsv. Each column represents one
instrumented run. The release benchmark measures separate quiet executions.

